<%#
    Everything the three top-level views put in <head> (#690).

    The block was identical in all three except for the <title>, so a change to
    it — the vendored-font <link> of #681, the favicon of #688, a CSP-driven
    move of a stylesheet — had to be made three times, and twice was enough to
    leave one view behind.

    Locals:

      title        required, the tab title, already assembled by the caller
      description  optional; the landing page is the only one search engines
                   see, and the dashboard pages sit behind a login

    Anything a single view needs on its own — guild-settings pulls in
    esc-html.js — stays in that view, after the include.
-%>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title><%= title %></title>
<%_ if (locals.description) { _%>
<meta name="description" content="<%= description %>">
<%_ } _%>
<%- include('favicon') %>
<%#
    The two faces that render above the fold on every page, asked for before the
    stylesheet that declares them (#945).

    Without these the font chain is three serial round trips deep — HTML, then
    fonts.css, then the woff2 — because a @font-face URL is not discoverable
    until the stylesheet declaring it has arrived and parsed. `font-display:
    swap` means that costs a flash of fallback text rather than blank space, but
    it is still two round trips of it. A preload moves the fetch to the first,
    alongside the stylesheets.

    Only the latin subsets of Inter Tight 400 and 600, and deliberately so: a
    preload for a face the page does not end up rendering is a wasted download
    that also warns in the console. 400 is the body text and 600 is every heading
    and label, on all three pages; every other weight, family and subset stays
    demand-loaded off its unicode-range exactly as before. Instrument Serif is
    left out for the same reason — it is display type, below the fold on the
    dashboard pages, and the landing page's wordmark is one line of it.

    `crossorigin` is not optional even though these are same-origin: fonts are
    fetched in CORS mode, and a preload whose mode does not match the fetch it is
    meant to satisfy is downloaded twice rather than once.

    The URLs are written plainly rather than through asset(), which is the one
    place in this file that is right. A preload only satisfies a later fetch when
    the two URLs match exactly, and the fetch comes from fonts.css, which names
    every face by bare filename with no `?v=` — the un-hashed font URLs of #903.
    Stamping a hash here would preload one URL and then fetch a different one:
    two downloads and a console warning instead of the round trip this saves.
    When #903 gives the faces hashed URLs, these two move to asset() with it.

    The two <link rel="stylesheet"> below are left as two. #945 also suggested
    collapsing them into one file, which the preload scanner has already made
    close to free — both are requested in the same round trip, neither waits on
    the other — and doing it would mean either editing the file
    scripts/fetch-fonts.sh regenerates or making the built image serve a
    different <head> from a plain checkout. Neither is worth a request that is
    already parallel.
-%>
<link rel="preload" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin href="/fonts/inter-tight-400-latin.woff2">
<link rel="preload" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin href="/fonts/inter-tight-600-latin.woff2">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="<%= asset('/fonts/fonts.css') %>">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="<%= asset('/styles.css') %>">
<%#
    The letter-avatar fallback for a Discord CDN image that does not load (#946).

    Every place the dashboard shows a guild icon or a user avatar already renders
    a letter tile when Discord reports no icon hash. What it could not handle was
    a hash that *is* reported and no longer resolves — a server that changed its
    icon since the OAuth session was minted, most often — which arrives as a 404
    and paints the browser's broken-image glyph in a layout built for a rounded
    tile. This turns that case into the same letter tile as having no icon at all.

    An `onerror=""` attribute is the obvious way to write this and is not
    available: the dashboard serves `script-src-attr 'none'` (#887), so an inline
    handler attribute is refused by the CSP whether it was authored here or
    injected. A capturing listener is the CSP-clean equivalent — `error` does not
    bubble, but it does capture — and it is registered here, in <head>, because
    it has to be installed before the parser reaches the first <img> it is meant
    to catch. A deferred external file would not run until after every image on
    the page had already had its chance to fail.

    Only images that opted in are touched. `data-fallback-class` names the tile
    class the markup would have rendered anyway and doubles as the opt-in flag,
    so an image with no fallback to swap to is left exactly as it is. It is
    cleared before the swap so that a second `error` on the same element — a
    browser that retries, a handler that somehow runs twice — cannot build two
    tiles out of one image.

    The letter goes in through `textContent` and never `innerHTML`: it is the
    first character of a guild or user name, which is attacker-controlled in
    exactly the way the rest of this dashboard's XSS work is about.

    The body below is kept terse deliberately. It is inline in a per-request HTML
    response, so unlike the files under public/ it is neither cached nor minified
    — every byte of it is re-sent on every page view, which is why the reasoning
    lives up here, in an EJS comment the renderer strips.
-%>
<script nonce="<%= locals.cspNonce %>">
document.addEventListener('error', function (event) {
    var img = event.target;
    if (!img || img.tagName !== 'IMG' || !img.dataset || !img.dataset.fallbackClass) return;
    var tile = document.createElement('div');
    tile.className = img.dataset.fallbackClass;
    delete img.dataset.fallbackClass;
    tile.textContent = img.dataset.fallbackText || '';
    img.replaceWith(tile);
}, true);
</script>
